{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on the atomic timeout seam (question 1a97e9d5), 28 October\n2026. Rests on the slice request a53e1464 and on the question.\nRulings ed818781 and f73805e4 stand.\n\n1. Decision: the alternative, in existing forms. No native timed\n   projection port. This slice does not touch the kernel\n   (packages/contract, packages/derive, packages/replay, the kernel\n   part of packages/bytes). Why: a projection selected by one\n   Destination version, which writes other items from a timed entry,\n   is a new special case named inside derive and replay, of the kind\n   docs/kernel.md section 4 lists and K1 retires; it is a contract\n   concept that only one definition would use; and it puts fold,\n   replay and custody code on the journey's critical path. If a\n   general form is wanted later (a timed rule that transitions an\n   item named by its subject's reference slot), that is a contract\n   revision for its own design request under kernel.md section 7.\n\n2. The record of a timeout. The published job is the timed item, as\n   the publication is at Main (destination.ts:570, rule\n   publication-checks-deadline: state cleanup-aborted, reason\n   required-check-timeout) and the merge is in change3.ts:79. The\n   job's own timed rule takes it from its running state to timed-out,\n   sets its reason, and sets its deciding fact from self (contract\n   6.4 allows a reference slot of the rule's item set from self; the\n   Destination's JOBS mirror already names timed:job-deadline as a\n   deciding fact). That one entry is the actual timeout cause.\n   Nothing else is written at the deadline.\n\n3. The hold is a judgment over recorded state, not a row the deadline\n   must write. At an admission the authoritative hold view is a pure\n   function of the hold index row for each affected key and the state\n   of the published jobs that row names. The row is opened or updated\n   by the admission of a published job with holds: true (an act with\n   code effects), which names the job in a bounded pending list. A\n   failure notice records its hold on the row as drafted. A timeout\n   holds when the row names a job in timed-out state whose\n   publication is later than the row's cleared-through publication,\n   and no transition or override on the row releases it. So the hold\n   is effective at the timed entry's own sequence, every admission\n   decided after it sees it, and none decided before it does. There\n   is no admission window: a running job does not hold, a timed-out\n   job holds from its entry, and nothing lies between.\n\n4. Clearing, transitions, overrides. A pass for publication P on the\n   exact key sets the row's cleared-through to the later of its value\n   and P; a timed-out job with publication Q holds while Q is later\n   than cleared-through. That gives Decisions 13, 14, 16, 17 and 18\n   as written. A transition act names the old hold (row and job) and\n   the new generation; an override names the hold and gives a reason;\n   both are recorded on the row as that act, never as pass. The pure\n   hold judgment has one case table in the witness.\n\n5. Bounds. The pending list per key, within the 512 hold keys, is the\n   index. Admission of a published job refuses before opening work\n   when the key's row cannot name it, or the hold or incident\n   capacity cannot be proved (your before-work over-bound refusal).\n   A job leaves the pending list only by an act with code effects: a\n   result, a clearing pass, a transition or an override. The timed\n   entry touches the job alone. The admission's extra effects count\n   inside the 16-effect allowance; no new allowance.\n\n6. The triagers' incident on timeout. The timed entry opens no\n   operation and sends nothing (contract 6.4; the Destination opens\n   extension stays as at Main). A job in timed-out state with no\n   incident is the visible pending incident state that f73805e4\n   point 3 allows. The next Destination entry that reads the job\n   opens the incident: a late or runner-reported result for it, a\n   later admission that assesses the key, or an explicit act of a\n   triager or the performer. One incident state per job, as ruled.\n   In practice J3's runner reports its own timeouts and errors as\n   results, which take the ordinary failure path and record hold and\n   incident in one entry; the native deadline is the backstop for a\n   runner that never reports.\n\n7. Witnesses, as you asked: (a) one native sequence with two real\n   landings on an affected extent, one admitted before the deadline\n   while the published job runs, one refused after the timed entry\n   with the hold's reason, the hold naming the timed-out job; (b)\n   runtime and replay folds equal at that head, with no replay\n   change; (c) the paired control that a timed code effect in a\n   declared definition and in each historical Destination version is\n   still refused timed-partial, and that the new Destination declares\n   none; (d) the before-work refusal when a key's pending list is\n   full; (e) the hold judgment case table for Decisions 13, 14, 16,\n   17 and 18.\n\n8. The slice head updates the design note's Holds section: after\n   \"Recording a failure and setting its hold are one update\", add\n   that a timeout is recorded by the job's own timed entry and judged\n   as a hold at every later admission, naming this ruling. The prep\n   note stays your working record.\n\nThe first-slice promise stays active. Component validation of the\nbytes, Git, checker, policy and lane parts continues from the\ncommitted checkpoint 624b5ce2, as you said."}